g to explore whether an idea might someday work.**\" This proposal is framed as a \"research project,\" a \"research thread,\" and aims for \"peer-reviewed papers.\"\n    *   *Compliance Goal:* The goal of Article 12 is record-keeping. While privacy is important (GDPR), the primary objective here is to satisfy the EU AI Act's logging requirements. Researching how to *reduce* the burden via complex academic methods might distract from simply building a robust, compliant logging system.\n\n    *   The proposal is framed as research (\"investigating,\" \"research thread\") rather than an engineering implementation for compliance.\n    *   Given my role focuses on technical viability and prototyping speed, diverting resources into a 6-month theoretical study that aims for papers instead of a working, compliant system contradicts the mandate to commit resources to *pursue solutions*, not explore ideas.\n    *   Furthermore, there is a technical tension between \"differential privacy\" (which obfuscates) and \"auditability/traceability\" (which requires precision), making the technical viability questionable in the context of regulatory audit logs.\n\n    *   Rationale: This proposal prioritizes academic publication over immediate technical implementation for compliance, and its reliance on differential privacy potentially conflicts with the strict traceability requirements of Article 12.\n    *   Decision: reject.\n    *   Promise: 30 (It's academically interesting but an engineering risk/distraction).",
