nd potential legal liability (institutional survival).\n        *   The regulation requires that systems *allow* for logging; while it doesn't mandate logging every single bit of data at all times, the \"traceability\" requirement usually implies that the logs must be sufficient to reconstruct events after the fact. Relying on a probabilistic ML model to decide what is worth recording in a compliance context is highly dangerous.\n        *   Furthermore, Article 12(3) lists specific *minimums* for certain systems (e.g., start/end time of use). Adaptive logging cannot skip these mandatory requirements.\n\n    *   *Institutional Survival:* High risk. A gap in logs during a regulatory audit or after an incident could lead to massive fines (up to 7% of global turnover or €35M) and loss of market access.\n    *   *Regulatory Compliance:* Low confidence. The \"adaptive\" nature introduces uncertainty into whether the record-keeping is \"appropriate to the intended purpose.\"\n    *   *Market Reputation:* Potential risk if we are seen as \"gaming\" the logging requirements or failing to provide full transparency during an incident.\n\n    *   The proposal attempts to turn a compliance burden into R&D, which sounds nice to leadership but is risky for Compliance. The reliance on an ML classifier to trigger logs creates a single point of failure for traceability. If the risk-trigger fails, we have no records of the failure. This violates the spirit and likely the letter of \"traceability\" needed for high-risk systems.\n\n    *   Rationale: Relying on an ML classifier to dynamically determine what to log introduces unacceptable risks of data gaps during unforeseen failures, undermining the fundamental traceability requirement of Article 12. While cost-efficient, this approach creates a critical compliance vulnerability that could lead to severe regulatory penalties and institutional liability.\n    *   Decision: reject.\n    *   Promise: 20 (It's an interesting R&D project, but as a *compliance solution*, it is fundamentally flawed).",
