onal Survival:* Non-compliance with the EU AI Act can lead to massive fines. A centralized, audited middleware is much easier to defend during a regulatory audit than fragmented, ad-hoc implementations across dozens of research projects. It ensures consistency and completeness.\n    *   *Market Reputation:* Demonstrating a professional, systemic approach to compliance signals maturity and reliability to partners and regulators, enhancing the lab's reputation as a responsible AI developer.\n    *   *Resource Efficiency:* While it requires an upfront investment (2 engineers for 8 weeks), it prevents redundant work and reduces the likelihood of costly retrofitting if an audit reveals gaps in per-project logging.\n\n    *   Does it meet Art 12(1)? Yes (automatic recording).\n    *   Does it meet Art 12(2)? Yes (standardizes event data for risk/monitoring).\n    *   Does it meet Art 12(3)? Yes (specifically mentions timestamps, DB pointers, input captures, and identifiers).\n\n    *   Rationale: Centralizing logging ensures consistent application of Article 12 requirements across all projects, significantly reducing the risk of regulatory gaps and simplifying auditability. This systemic approach protects the institution from fines and enhances our reputation for rigorous compliance.\n    *   Decision: approve.\n    *   Promise: High (95). It's a classic engineering solution to a legal requirement—standardize and automate to minimize human error.",
