ectly.\n\n    *   The EU AI Act requires *post-market monitoring* (Article 72) and risk management. This proposal essentially operationalizes post-market monitoring into a research project.\n    *   Recital 71 emphasizes the need for \"comprehensible information on how high-risk AI systems... perform throughout their lifetime.\" The proposal aligns with this spirit.\n    *   *Risk:* Publicly publishing findings about failures in *our own* deployed biometric systems is risky from a legal/liability perspective. If we publish that a system drifted, we are effectively documenting our own potential non-compliance or system failure for the public record.\n\n    *   The proposal attempts to \"weaponize\" compliance data for research. While intellectually appealing, as a Compliance Officer, my priority isn't \"scientific contribution\"—it's reducing risk.\n    *   Publicly disclosing that we are monitoring \"novel failures\" and \"edge-case accumulation\" in high-risk biometric systems creates a discovery trail for litigants and regulators.\n    *   Wait—the proposal says \"anonymised aggregate findings.\" This mitigates some risk, but not the systemic risk of admitting failure patterns.\n    *   However, from a *market reputation* standpoint, being seen as the gold standard for transparency and reliability (by actually studying it) can be a massive competitive advantage in the EU market, where \"Trustworthy AI\" is the key selling point.\n\n    *   Does this help survival? It might attract regulatory scrutiny, but ignoring drift leads to catastrophic failure (and total loss of survival).\n    *   Does this ensure compliance? Yes, it forces a disciplined approach to Art 12 logs.\n    *   Does this enhance reputation? Potentially, if the \"scientific contribution\" outweighs the \"admission of failure.\"\n\n    *Critique:* The proposal specifically targets *biometric systems*. Biometrics are high-sensitivity under the AI Act and GDPR. Publishing anything about their failures