t the budget, it doesn't actually help me *implement* the record-keeping required by Article 12. Moreover, phasing out projects simply because they require architectural rework for logging could kill technically viable and scientifically valuable prototypes that just need better engineering leadership or a different logging architecture.\n    *   *Crucial Point:* The proposal is about *funding triage*, not technical implementation. As a Development Engineer concerned with viability and prototyping speed, this feels like an administrative overhead exercise (PIs writing memos) and a risk to project longevity based on initial compliance estimates. It doesn't offer a technical path forward; it offers a way to stop doing things that are \"hard\" to make compliant.\n\n    *   Does it help me build the logs? No.\n    *   Does it improve prototyping quality/speed? No, it adds bureaucratic overhead (memos) and potential project cancellation.\n    *   Is it technically viable? It's a management process, so \"viability\" is different here, but from an engineering standpoint, it doesn't solve the Article 12 requirement; it just avoids projects that are hard to fix.\n\n    *   *Rationale:* This proposal is a budgetary and administrative triage mechanism rather than a technical solution for implementing record-keeping. It risks canceling scientifically valuable prototypes based on initial cost estimates without providing any actual engineering framework to achieve compliance.\n    *   *Decision:* Reject.\n    *   *Promise:* Low (maybe 10-20) because while it manages budget, it doesn't solve the technical problem and creates friction for researchers/engineers.",
