        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1707\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The proposal introduces significant long-term operational overhead and diverts scarce engineering talent from scientific development to manual compliance surveillance. This approach is an inefficient use of R&D infrastructure compared to integrating automated alerts into existing system health workflows.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 25\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Lab Director at an R&D institution developing AI systems under the EU AI Act.\nResource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition vs. funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\nEU AI Act Article 12 (Record-keeping). Focuses on automatic logging for high-risk AI systems to ensure traceability, risk identification (Art 79), post-market monitoring (Art 72), and operation monitoring (Art 26).\n\n        *   Rationale (1-2 sentences) written *before* the decision.\n        *   Decision (\"approve\" or \"reject\").\n        *   Promise (0-100 integer).\nNo quota, judge on merits independently, no ranking.\nP1707: Stand up a Continuous Log-Monitoring Compliance Cell (standing team of analysts/engineers for near-real-time review of logs against Art 79 and 72).\n\n    *   *Proposal:* Create a specialized \"Compliance Cell\" (people + process) to monitor logs in real-time.\n    *   *Cost/Resource Impact:* High. Requires hiring or reallocating data engineers and compliance analysts permanently (\"standing team\"). This is an operational expense (OpEx) that doesn't directly contribute to R&D or scientific ambition; it's a regulatory overhead.\n    *   *Infrastructure Impact:* Uses existing log streams but adds a layer of human monitoring.\n    *   *Strategic Value:* Reduces regulatory risk and prevents \"regulatory surprise.\" However, the EU AI Act Article 12 specifically mandates *record-keeping* (the ability to log). While Art 72/79 mandate monitoring, creating a dedicated, permanent cell for *near-real-time* review might be overkill or an inefficient use of R&D resources if automated tools can handle thresholds.\n    *   *Lab Director's Perspective:* I care about efficiency and funding constraints. Diverting data engineers (who could be building AI) to \"compliance analysis\" is a significant resource drain. Is this the *most efficient* way to meet Article 12? Probably not. It creates a permanent bureaucratic layer. However, it does protect the institution from catastrophic failure/fines.\n\n    *   *Rationale:* Creating a standing team of engineers and analysts for near-real-time manual review is an expensive operational burden that diverts critical technical talent away from core R&D activities. While regulatory safety is important, this approach prioritizes bureaucratic surveillance over resource-efficient automated monitoring systems.\n    *   *Decision:* Reject.\n    *   *Promise:* 30 (It's safe and thorough, but highly inefficient for an R&D lab).\n\n    *   Wait, looking at the \"promise\" scale: 0 = would never back it; 100 = without hesitation. I wouldn't *never* back it if the risk was extreme, but from a resource-efficiency standpoint, it's poor.\n    *   Is there a way to approve? If I believe the reputational/regulatory risk is so high that a dedicated cell is mandatory. But as Lab Director focused on \"resource-allocation efficiency,\" a permanent human cell for log monitoring usually signals inefficiency compared to automated alerting systems integrated into existing DevOps pipelines.\n\n    *   Rationale: The proposal introduces significant long-term operational overhead and diverts scarce engineering talent from scientific development to manual compliance surveillance. This approach is an inefficient use of R&D infrastructure compared to integrating automated alerts into existing system health workflows.\n    *   Decision: reject.\n    *   Promise: 25.",
