iction.\n    *   *Regulatory Alignment:* It directly addresses Recital 71 (technical documentation). It doesn't specifically address Article 12's *logging* requirements (which are about event logs), but rather the broader technical documentation mentioned in Recital 71.\n    *   *Resource Commitment:* This is a significant commitment: drafting, piloting for six months, dealing with standardization bodies, and building tooling.\n\n    *   As a Development Engineer concerned with *speed and quality of prototyping*, does this help?\n    *   Standardized schemas actually *improve* the quality of documentation (which is often an afterthought) and make it easier to automate compliance checks. If we have machine-readable records, we can automate parts of the \"keep up to date\" requirement in Recital 71.\n    *   However, the scope is very broad (\"standards effort,\" \"European standardisation body\"). This feels more like a policy/academic pursuit than an engineering solution for immediate viability and prototyping speed.\n    *   Wait, look at it from the perspective of *technical viability*: Is it viable? Yes. Does it improve quality? Yes. But does it help me prototype faster *today*? Not really; it adds a layer of formalization.\n    *   Conversely, if we are building high-risk systems, the documentation burden is massive. Automating this via schemas is the only way to maintain \"up to date\" records without grinding development to a halt.\n\n    *   Recital 71: \"...documentation should be kept up to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system.\"\n    *   Manual documentation kills prototyping speed. Machine-readable, schema-based documentation allows for automation (e.g., extracting training parameters directly from config files into a compliance record). This is a huge win for a Dev Engineer.\n\n    *   *Rationale:* Automating technical documentation via machine-readable schemas reduces the manual overhead of maintaining compliance records during r