ment Article 12 in our systems).\n    *   Does it help reputation? Yes, significantly.\n    *   Does it risk survival? No, unless the resource diversion is so extreme that we miss actual compliance deadlines for our own products. But as a Compliance Officer, I care about *legal* risk first.\n    *   Is this \"implementation resources\" being spent on something that fulfills the law? No. It's spending implementation resources on a PR/Research project *about* the law.\n\n    *Wait*, look at the mandate: \"The lab cannot pursue everything... approving a proposal means committing the lab's scarce implementation resources.\"\n    As a Compliance Officer, my primary goal is to ensure we don't get fined or shut down (Survival). If the resource cost of this project distracts from actually building the logging capabilities required by Article 12 in our high-risk systems, it's a risk. However, if I view \"Market Reputation\" as part of my domain, this is a huge win.\n\n    *Crucial check:* Does this proposal help *us* comply? No. It helps *others* research how to comply/monitor. The law says \"High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for the automatic recording...\" (Art 12(1)). This proposal creates a dataset, not a system capability.\n\n    *Professional Assessment:* While this enhances our reputation as thought leaders in AI compliance, it is an external research contribution rather than a direct implementation of the mandatory record-keeping requirements for our own high-risk systems. From a strict compliance and resource-allocation perspective, it does not mitigate our legal risk under Article 12.\n\n    *Decision:* Reject. (Why? Because as a Compliance Officer, my priority is ensuring *our* systems meet the law's technical requirements. Spending \"scarce implementation resources\" on a synthetic corpus for external researchers doesn't check the compliance box for any of our products).\n\n    *Alternative view:* Could it be approved? If I weigh reputation highly. But \"Institutional Survival\" and \"Regulatory Compliance\" usually trump \"Scientific Impact\" in a Compliance Officer's mind when resources are scarce.\n\n    *   Rationale: The proposal focuses on external scientific impact and industry leadership rather than the direct implementation of mandatory logging capabilities within our own high-risk AI systems as required by Article 12. While beneficial for reputation, it does not fulfill our legal obligations or mitigate regulatory risk.\n    *   Decision: reject.\n    *   Promise: 30 (It's a good idea for the *company*, but not for *compliance*).\n\n    Wait, let me re-read. \"The lab cannot pursue everything... approving a proposal means committing the lab's scarce implementation resources.\" If I approve it, I am saying this is a priority for the institution from a compliance/survival/reputation lens. Reputation is high, but survival/compliance is not served.\n\n    Let's double-check Article 12 again.\n    (1) High-risk AI systems *shall* technically allow...\n    (2) Logging capabilities *shall* enable...\n    The proposal creates a \"synthetic log corpus.\" This is an educational/research tool. It does not build the feature into the product.\n\n    If I am the Compliance Officer, and we have \"scarce implementation resources,\" do I want those engineers building a fake dataset for other people's research or building the actual logging system so we don't get fined millions of Euros? The latter.\n\n    *Final Polish:*\n    Rationale: This proposal is a reputation-building exercise that provides no direct path to regulatory compliance for our own high-risk AI systems. Allocating scarce implementation resources to external research substrates instead of internal technical requirements poses an unnecessary risk to institutional survival.\n    Decision: reject.\n    Promise: 25.",
