m*. It does *not* explicitly demand \"indefinite\" storage if the system is decommissioned, nor does it necessarily require all logs to be in a high-cost online query layer forever. Most compliance regimes allow for tiered storage (hot $\\rightarrow$ cold/archive).\n\n    *   The proposal aims for efficiency by centralizing infrastructure (Positive).\n    *   However, the commitment to \"indefinite\" retention without TTL or garbage collection creates an unmanaged, growing financial liability (Negative).\n    *   As Lab Director, I cannot approve a blanket mandate for unbounded storage costs. While the *centralization* is good, the *retention policy* described is fiscally irresponsible and potentially overkill for many systems.\n    *   The claim that prototypes \"cannot demonstrate lifetime traceability\" without this specific shared layer is an exaggeration; they just need *a* way to store logs, not necessarily a permanent gRPC-powered time-series store with zero garbage collection.\n\n    *   *Rationale:* Centralizing the logging infrastructure avoids redundant effort across projects, but the commitment to indefinite retention without TTLs creates an unsustainable and unbounded cost liability for the lab's budget.\n    *   *Decision:* Reject (due to the \"indefinite/no GC\" part which violates resource efficiency). Wait—should I approve it if I think it *could* be tweaked? No, the prompt says \"approve every proposal that clears your bar... reject every proposal that does not.\" The specific technical commitment here is a fiscal disaster.\n\n    *Alternative thought:* Is it too harsh to reject based on storage costs? In an R&D lab, managing the cloud bill is often the #1 constraint on scientific ambition. Unbounded growth in a \"shared layer\" for *all* high-risk systems will eventually crash the budget.\n\n    *Refining Decision:* The proposal's approach to infrastructure (shared layer) is correct, but its operational strategy (no TTL/GC) is an unacceptable resource drain. Since I cannot edit the proposal and must decide on it as written: Reject.\n\n    Wait, let me look at the \"promise\" score. It's a good idea in *principle* (centralization), but flawed in *execution* (cost). The promise might be medium-high if we ignore the cost, but from my vantage point (Resource Allocation), it's dangerous.\n\n    Actually, looking closer: \"rebuilding ad-hoc storage per project would stall every new high-risk release.\" This is a strong argument for the *shared* part. But as Lab Director, I can't sign off on \"disable garbage collection.\"\n\n    Let's consider if there is a middle ground. If I reject it, we go back to ad-hoc storage? No, this is an ideation exercise. Other proposals might suggest centralized storage *with* reasonable retention policies. This specific proposal (P2165) explicitly mandates \"indefinite\" and \"no TTL.\" That's the dealbreaker.\n\n    *Rationale:* While centralizing logging infrastructure avoids redundant engineering effort across projects, the mandate for indefinite retention without garbage collection creates an unbounded financial liability that threatens our overall budget efficiency.\n    *Decision:* reject.\n    *Promise:* 40 (The architectural approach is sound, but the cost management is nonexistent).\n\n    Let's double check Article 12 again. \"over the lifetime of the system.\" If a system lasts 10 years, logs must exist for 10 years. Does that mean they must be in an *active query layer*? No. Archive storage (S3 Glacier, etc.) is much cheaper than a \"time-series store behind an internal API\" with no GC. The proposal's technical choice is inefficient.",
