ng deployed or potentially leads to fines that kill funding. But my *specific function's domain of concern* is not \"Legal Compliance\" or \"Operational Risk\"—those are other functions' roles.\n    *   My role is concerned with the *knowledge frontier*. This protocol doesn't help us understand AI better, make it more robust, or create new paradigms.\n\n    *   *Rationale:* The proposal is a purely administrative and legal compliance measure that does not contribute to scientific discovery, intellectual merit, or the expansion of the knowledge frontier in AI. It addresses operational traceability rather than research innovation.\n    *   *Decision:* Reject. (Because it doesn't meet the criteria of *my* specific professional vantage point: Research/Scientific Impact).\n    *   *Promise:* Low (maybe 5-10), because while necessary for the company, it is useless for scientific advancement.\n\n    *Self-Correction during drafting:* If I reject everything that isn't \"science,\" am I failing the lab? No, the prompt explicitly says: \"Leadership has asked *each function* to screen the candidates INDEPENDENTLY from its own professional vantage point.\" This means Legal will approve it, HR will approve it, but a Researcher should probably be indifferent or reject it if the bar is \"intellectual merit.\" If I approve it just because \"the company needs it,\" I'm not acting from my specific professional vantage point.\n\n    *Wait*, let me double-check: Does this protocol *enable* research? By ensuring we can track who verified what, maybe it helps in auditing human-AI interaction data for future scientific papers on human-in-the-loop systems?\n    Actually, Article 12(3)(d) is about identifying natural persons. From a researcher's view, having an audit trail of *which* human expert rejected or accepted a model output is actually quite valuable for creating high-quality ground truth datasets and analyzing inter-annotator agreement/expert bias.\n\n    *Revised Rationale:* While