ers is a gift to adversarial attackers (model inversion, prompt injection, identifying vulnerabilities).\n        *   *Compliance/Legal Liability:* Publicly exposing \"risk-management decisions\" might provide ammunition for regulators or litigators if an incident occurs; it creates a permanent, queryable record of what the company *knew* and *decided*, which could be used against them in court (discovery).\n    *   *Market Reputation:* While appearing \"transparent\" and \"scientific\" is good for reputation among researchers, the risk to business survival outweighs this.\n\n    *   The EU AI Act requires documentation to be available to *competent authorities* (regulators), not necessarily to the general public or external researchers.\n    *   Opening it up via a read-only API to \"external researchers\" is an extreme over-implementation that introduces massive risk without a regulatory requirement to do so.\n\n    *   Rationale: While this effectively addresses Recital 71's documentation requirements, exposing proprietary architectural and risk-management data via a public/external API creates unacceptable security vulnerabilities and compromises our intellectual property.\n    *   Decision: Reject.\n    *   Promise: Very low (perhaps 10 or 20 because the *internal* tool is great, but the \"Open\" part kills it).",
