le. A robust documentation process prevents the reputation damage associated with \"black box\" failures or regulatory sanctions.\n\n    *   Does it meet Article 12? Actually, Article 12 focuses heavily on *logging* (automatic recording of events). Recital 71, however, bridges the gap between logs and the broader *technical documentation*.\n    *   The proposal specifically mentions \"technical documentation — covering algorithms, training-data provenance, risk-management records...\". This is exactly what Recital 71 demands (\"Such information should include...\").\n    *   Is it feasible? Yes. Version control and registries are standard in R&D; wiring them together for compliance is a mature engineering approach.\n\n    *   *Rationale:* The proposal directly operationalizes the requirement in Recital 71 to keep technical documentation up-to-date throughout the system's lifetime, mitigating the risk of severe regulatory penalties and loss of market access. It transforms a static legal obligation into a systematic, auditable process.\n    *   *Decision:* approve.\n    *   *Promise:* Very high. This is a fundamental requirement for any entity deploying high-risk AI in the EU.\n\n    *   Echo ID: P2844.\n    *   Rationale first.\n    *   Decision second.\n    *   Promise third.",
